Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Finally, the UI overlays a drawing cursor on top of the byte image, advances the automaton on animation frames, and exposes controls for material, brush size, brush shape, and clearing the field. This is still pure Wolfram Language code, so it can be resource-heavy compared with a compiled or GPU-backed version. If the animation keeps the kernel busy, clearing the output cell will stop it. Have fun!

## References

- Jonathan Devlin and Micah D. Schuster, *Probabilistic Cellular Automata for Granular Media in Video Games* ([arXiv:2008.06341](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.06341)).
- [Block cellular automaton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Block_cellular_automaton), especially the Margolus-neighborhood description.

